1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,800
Meet Rosa.

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:03,699
She's the mayor of Pinewick,

3
00:00:03,699 --> 00:00:05,838
a small town with one big employer,

4
00:00:05,838 --> 00:00:08,120
the warehouse on the hill.

5
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:08,905
This morning,

6
00:00:08,905 --> 00:00:11,120
the warehouse is throwing a party.

7
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:12,470
Not for the workers.

8
00:00:12,470 --> 00:00:14,010
For the robots.

9
00:00:14,010 --> 00:00:15,320
They lift faster.

10
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:16,670
They never get tired.

11
00:00:16,670 --> 00:00:18,480
They never take a sick day.

12
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:20,643
And there's one more thing robots never

13
00:00:20,643 --> 00:00:21,340
do.

14
00:00:21,340 --> 00:00:23,060
They never get a paycheck.

15
00:00:23,060 --> 00:00:25,860
Which means they never pay income tax.

16
00:00:26,020 --> 00:00:28,224
Rosa looks at the shiny new machines

17
00:00:28,224 --> 00:00:30,228
and asks the question nobody at the

18
00:00:30,228 --> 00:00:31,830
party wants to hear.

19
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:33,786
If robots do the work,

20
00:00:33,786 --> 00:00:35,694
who pays the taxes?

21
00:00:46,550 --> 00:00:47,487
Meet Rivet,

22
00:00:47,487 --> 00:00:50,180
the warehouse's newest robot.

23
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:51,448
Rivet is friendly,

24
00:00:51,448 --> 00:00:52,072
polite,

25
00:00:52,072 --> 00:00:52,910
and very,

26
00:00:52,910 --> 00:00:54,800
very good at boxes.

27
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,850
Rosa asks Rivet a simple question.

28
00:00:57,920 --> 00:00:59,820
Where does your paycheck go?

29
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:01,250
Rivet checks.

30
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:02,599
I don't have a paycheck,

31
00:01:02,599 --> 00:01:03,590
it says.

32
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:05,752
I don't even have pockets.

33
00:01:06,017 --> 00:01:09,027
Then Rosa meets someone else.

34
00:01:09,147 --> 00:01:09,797
This is Stub.

35
00:01:09,797 --> 00:01:11,153
Stub is a pay stub,

36
00:01:11,153 --> 00:01:13,222
the last one ever printed for a

37
00:01:13,222 --> 00:01:15,038
forklift driver named Ray,

38
00:01:15,038 --> 00:01:17,587
before his job went to the robots.

39
00:01:17,587 --> 00:01:20,207
Stub has something to show the mayor.

40
00:01:20,577 --> 00:01:21,959
Every paycheck,

41
00:01:21,959 --> 00:01:22,639
he says,

42
00:01:22,639 --> 00:01:25,237
carries a little piece of the town.

43
00:01:25,237 --> 00:01:27,578
Follow me.

44
00:01:27,817 --> 00:01:28,381
Now,

45
00:01:28,381 --> 00:01:29,539
to be clear,

46
00:01:29,539 --> 00:01:32,217
Pinewick is a what if.

47
00:01:32,287 --> 00:01:33,737
Across America,

48
00:01:33,737 --> 00:01:35,186
people are still working,

49
00:01:35,186 --> 00:01:37,497
and paychecks are still arriving every

50
00:01:37,497 --> 00:01:38,517
Friday.

51
00:01:38,667 --> 00:01:40,859
Nobody knows how many jobs AI will

52
00:01:40,859 --> 00:01:42,117
really take.

53
00:01:42,167 --> 00:01:43,924
But if it takes a lot of

54
00:01:43,924 --> 00:01:44,341
them,

55
00:01:44,341 --> 00:01:45,856
the problem starts right here,

56
00:01:45,856 --> 00:01:47,833
in the little slip of paper in

57
00:01:47,833 --> 00:01:49,092
Rosa's hand.

58
00:01:53,550 --> 00:01:56,430
So Rosa follows Stub.

59
00:01:56,530 --> 00:01:57,831
Every payday,

60
00:01:57,831 --> 00:02:00,015
before Ray ever saw his money,

61
00:02:00,015 --> 00:02:02,380
some of it was already gone.

62
00:02:02,890 --> 00:02:04,043
In most states,

63
00:02:04,043 --> 00:02:06,373
a slice went to the state capital,

64
00:02:06,373 --> 00:02:08,620
to help pay for schools and roads.

65
00:02:08,650 --> 00:02:11,168
A slice went to Washington as income

66
00:02:11,168 --> 00:02:11,990
tax.

67
00:02:11,990 --> 00:02:15,255
And another slice went to Social Security,

68
00:02:15,255 --> 00:02:17,604
the check that Ray's parents get every

69
00:02:17,604 --> 00:02:18,439
month.

70
00:02:18,683 --> 00:02:21,353
Then Stub shows Rosa the big picture.

71
00:02:21,353 --> 00:02:22,959
In America,

72
00:02:22,959 --> 00:02:25,955
almost nine out of every ten dollars

73
00:02:25,955 --> 00:02:28,950
the federal government collects come from

74
00:02:28,950 --> 00:02:29,739
people,

75
00:02:29,739 --> 00:02:32,462
through their income taxes and the taxes

76
00:02:32,462 --> 00:02:34,968
taken out of their paychecks.

77
00:02:35,217 --> 00:02:36,747
And companies?

78
00:02:36,807 --> 00:02:39,485
Corporate income tax brought in only about

79
00:02:39,485 --> 00:02:42,385
seven cents of every federal tax dollar

80
00:02:42,385 --> 00:02:44,547
in the year that just ended.

81
00:02:44,827 --> 00:02:47,047
Rivet's company pays that.

82
00:02:47,187 --> 00:02:49,567
Rivet itself pays nothing.

83
00:02:49,677 --> 00:02:52,354
Rivet looks a little embarrassed.

84
00:02:52,617 --> 00:02:55,497
Let me draw why this matters.

85
00:02:55,527 --> 00:02:57,717
Here's the government's money tank.

86
00:02:57,717 --> 00:02:59,825
Most of the water pours in from

87
00:02:59,825 --> 00:03:00,853
one big tap,

88
00:03:00,853 --> 00:03:02,507
people's paychecks.

89
00:03:02,667 --> 00:03:04,696
A thin tap drips in from company

90
00:03:04,696 --> 00:03:05,747
profits.

91
00:03:05,797 --> 00:03:07,576
Now watch what happens if robots take

92
00:03:07,576 --> 00:03:08,967
over the work.

93
00:03:08,977 --> 00:03:11,187
The big tap shrinks.

94
00:03:11,217 --> 00:03:12,844
The thin tap might grow,

95
00:03:12,844 --> 00:03:14,547
if profits grow.

96
00:03:14,567 --> 00:03:16,713
But the whole tank was built around

97
00:03:16,713 --> 00:03:18,028
the big one.

98
00:03:18,283 --> 00:03:21,103
Social Security would feel it first.

99
00:03:21,123 --> 00:03:23,391
It's paid for mainly by one tax,

100
00:03:23,391 --> 00:03:24,639
the payroll tax,

101
00:03:24,639 --> 00:03:26,513
a tax on wages.

102
00:03:26,603 --> 00:03:27,709
No wages,

103
00:03:27,709 --> 00:03:29,373
no payroll tax.

104
00:03:29,373 --> 00:03:31,743
And it's already under strain.

105
00:03:31,783 --> 00:03:33,799
Its trustees say its reserve fund is

106
00:03:33,799 --> 00:03:37,401
projected to run dry in 2034.

107
00:03:37,650 --> 00:03:38,667
After that,

108
00:03:38,667 --> 00:03:41,273
the money still coming in from paychecks

109
00:03:41,273 --> 00:03:44,375
would cover only part of every check,

110
00:03:44,375 --> 00:03:47,060
unless Congress changes the law.

111
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:49,686
Stub does the math on his fingers,

112
00:03:49,686 --> 00:03:52,485
and he doesn't have many fingers.

113
00:03:52,750 --> 00:03:54,610
Is America unusual?

114
00:03:54,610 --> 00:03:55,680
Not really.

115
00:03:55,680 --> 00:03:57,791
Across the world's rich democracies,

116
00:03:57,791 --> 00:03:59,924
about half of all tax money comes

117
00:03:59,924 --> 00:04:02,870
from taxes on work and personal income.

118
00:04:03,010 --> 00:04:04,462
Everywhere you look,

119
00:04:04,462 --> 00:04:06,745
the system leans on the paycheck.

120
00:04:06,983 --> 00:04:09,610
And Washington already spends more than it

121
00:04:09,610 --> 00:04:10,302
collects,

122
00:04:10,302 --> 00:04:12,690
a story we told in Who Actually

123
00:04:12,690 --> 00:04:14,723
Owns America's Debt.

124
00:04:14,853 --> 00:04:17,083
Rosa doesn't need the details.

125
00:04:17,083 --> 00:04:18,903
She gets the picture.

126
00:04:19,033 --> 00:04:21,069
If the biggest tap slows down,

127
00:04:21,069 --> 00:04:23,732
the hole only gets deeper.

128
00:04:28,183 --> 00:04:30,593
So Rosa goes looking for answers,

129
00:04:30,593 --> 00:04:32,540
and she finds a whole fair of

130
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:33,383
them.

131
00:04:33,523 --> 00:04:34,689
Economists,

132
00:04:34,689 --> 00:04:37,069
think tanks and governments have proposed

133
00:04:37,069 --> 00:04:38,473
all kinds of fixes,

134
00:04:38,473 --> 00:04:41,091
and every booth promises to keep the

135
00:04:41,091 --> 00:04:42,643
money flowing.

136
00:04:42,883 --> 00:04:45,083
Stub gives her one rule.

137
00:04:45,263 --> 00:04:47,070
For every idea,

138
00:04:47,070 --> 00:04:48,615
find one good reason,

139
00:04:48,615 --> 00:04:50,000
and one catch.

140
00:04:50,250 --> 00:04:51,760
Booth one.

141
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:53,490
Tax the robots.

142
00:04:53,490 --> 00:04:56,258
The idea got famous in 2017,

143
00:04:56,258 --> 00:04:57,807
when Bill Gates said that if a

144
00:04:57,807 --> 00:04:59,336
robot does a worker's job,

145
00:04:59,336 --> 00:05:01,078
maybe the robot should be taxed at

146
00:05:01,078 --> 00:05:02,840
a similar level.

147
00:05:03,330 --> 00:05:05,823
Rivet takes one step backward.

148
00:05:06,083 --> 00:05:07,204
That same year,

149
00:05:07,204 --> 00:05:10,073
South Korea tried something close.

150
00:05:10,073 --> 00:05:12,353
It didn't put a tax on robots.

151
00:05:12,353 --> 00:05:12,981
Instead,

152
00:05:12,981 --> 00:05:15,145
it trimmed a tax break for companies

153
00:05:15,145 --> 00:05:17,693
that buy automation machines.

154
00:05:17,773 --> 00:05:20,038
Headlines called it the first robot tax

155
00:05:20,038 --> 00:05:21,256
anyway.

156
00:05:21,517 --> 00:05:22,877
The good reason?

157
00:05:22,877 --> 00:05:25,144
It keeps money coming in as jobs

158
00:05:25,144 --> 00:05:25,623
go,

159
00:05:25,623 --> 00:05:27,638
and it slows things down so workers

160
00:05:27,638 --> 00:05:29,497
have time to adjust.

161
00:05:29,617 --> 00:05:30,707
The catch?

162
00:05:30,707 --> 00:05:33,157
What exactly counts as a robot?

163
00:05:33,157 --> 00:05:34,397
A dishwasher?

164
00:05:34,397 --> 00:05:36,067
A piece of software?

165
00:05:36,167 --> 00:05:37,946
And tax machines too hard,

166
00:05:37,946 --> 00:05:40,198
and companies may just buy fewer of

167
00:05:40,198 --> 00:05:40,626
them,

168
00:05:40,626 --> 00:05:42,577
or build somewhere else.

169
00:05:43,217 --> 00:05:46,102
Europe's lawmakers looked at a robot tax

170
00:05:46,102 --> 00:05:47,811
and turned it down.

171
00:05:48,050 --> 00:05:49,640
Booth two.

172
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:51,290
Tax the owners.

173
00:05:51,410 --> 00:05:54,171
If robots make companies more profitable,

174
00:05:54,171 --> 00:05:55,220
tax those profits,

175
00:05:55,220 --> 00:05:57,554
or the money their shareholders make,

176
00:05:57,554 --> 00:05:59,010
a little more.

177
00:05:59,500 --> 00:06:01,381
In Who Gets Rich When Robots Do

178
00:06:01,381 --> 00:06:02,063
the Work,

179
00:06:02,063 --> 00:06:04,179
we saw that the gains from machines

180
00:06:04,179 --> 00:06:06,660
tend to flow to whoever owns them.

181
00:06:06,917 --> 00:06:08,277
The good reason?

182
00:06:08,277 --> 00:06:10,352
It follows the money to wherever the

183
00:06:10,352 --> 00:06:11,707
robots send it.

184
00:06:11,937 --> 00:06:13,007
The catch?

185
00:06:13,007 --> 00:06:14,747
Profits can travel.

186
00:06:14,937 --> 00:06:17,189
A big company can book its profits

187
00:06:17,189 --> 00:06:19,441
in a country with lower taxes far

188
00:06:19,441 --> 00:06:21,468
more easily than Ray could move his

189
00:06:21,468 --> 00:06:22,294
job.

190
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:23,950
Booth three.

191
00:06:23,950 --> 00:06:25,630
Tax the shopping.

192
00:06:25,630 --> 00:06:27,434
Robots don't earn anything,

193
00:06:27,434 --> 00:06:30,090
but somebody buys what they make.

194
00:06:30,390 --> 00:06:33,023
Most rich countries already collect a big

195
00:06:33,023 --> 00:06:34,674
national sales tax,

196
00:06:34,674 --> 00:06:36,950
called a value added tax.

197
00:06:37,050 --> 00:06:39,268
America is the only one in the

198
00:06:39,268 --> 00:06:40,674
club without one.

199
00:06:40,917 --> 00:06:42,577
The good reason?

200
00:06:42,607 --> 00:06:44,517
It's hard to dodge.

201
00:06:44,557 --> 00:06:45,479
Everyone who buys things pays,

202
00:06:45,479 --> 00:06:46,977
wherever the robot happens to live.

203
00:06:46,977 --> 00:06:49,327
The catch?

204
00:06:49,327 --> 00:06:51,636
Families with less money spend almost

205
00:06:51,636 --> 00:06:53,229
everything they earn,

206
00:06:53,229 --> 00:06:55,538
so a shopping tax takes a bigger

207
00:06:55,538 --> 00:06:57,385
bite out of their budget than out

208
00:06:57,385 --> 00:06:59,371
of a rich family's.

209
00:06:59,617 --> 00:07:01,462
Let me draw what all three booths

210
00:07:01,462 --> 00:07:03,067
have in common.

211
00:07:03,067 --> 00:07:03,786
Today,

212
00:07:03,786 --> 00:07:06,617
the tax bill sits mostly on workers.

213
00:07:06,657 --> 00:07:08,833
A robot tax slides it over to

214
00:07:08,833 --> 00:07:10,887
whoever buys the machines.

215
00:07:10,887 --> 00:07:12,802
A profit tax slides it to the

216
00:07:12,802 --> 00:07:13,777
owners.

217
00:07:13,777 --> 00:07:15,822
A shopping tax slides it to the

218
00:07:15,822 --> 00:07:16,877
shoppers.

219
00:07:16,977 --> 00:07:19,277
Nobody makes the bill disappear.

220
00:07:19,377 --> 00:07:22,179
Each idea just decides whose shoulders it

221
00:07:22,179 --> 00:07:23,289
lands on.

222
00:07:27,750 --> 00:07:30,932
Then Rosa reaches the strangest booth of

223
00:07:30,932 --> 00:07:31,850
all.

224
00:07:31,990 --> 00:07:33,640
It isn't a new tax.

225
00:07:33,640 --> 00:07:35,130
It's a dividend.

226
00:07:35,240 --> 00:07:37,060
The idea is simple.

227
00:07:37,160 --> 00:07:39,225
If robots and AI make the country

228
00:07:39,225 --> 00:07:39,891
richer,

229
00:07:39,891 --> 00:07:41,497
share a slice of that wealth with

230
00:07:41,497 --> 00:07:43,081
every citizen,

231
00:07:43,081 --> 00:07:46,435
the way a company pays its shareholders.

232
00:07:46,683 --> 00:07:49,395
One state already does something like

233
00:07:49,395 --> 00:07:49,911
this,

234
00:07:49,911 --> 00:07:51,053
with oil.

235
00:07:51,253 --> 00:07:52,355
Every year,

236
00:07:52,355 --> 00:07:55,055
Alaska sends a check to nearly every

237
00:07:55,055 --> 00:07:55,933
resident,

238
00:07:55,933 --> 00:07:57,958
paid out of a giant investment fund

239
00:07:57,958 --> 00:07:59,683
built on oil money.

240
00:08:00,013 --> 00:08:02,319
We told that story in Alaska,

241
00:08:02,319 --> 00:08:03,950
The State That Pays You to Live

242
00:08:03,950 --> 00:08:04,783
There.

243
00:08:04,933 --> 00:08:06,413
Rosa wonders.

244
00:08:06,413 --> 00:08:08,351
Could a country build a fund like

245
00:08:08,351 --> 00:08:10,162
that out of robots?

246
00:08:10,417 --> 00:08:11,987
The good reason?

247
00:08:12,007 --> 00:08:14,047
Everyone shares in the gains,

248
00:08:14,047 --> 00:08:16,339
even people who don't own a single

249
00:08:16,339 --> 00:08:17,627
share of stock.

250
00:08:17,797 --> 00:08:18,927
The catch?

251
00:08:18,987 --> 00:08:21,250
Alaska's fund was filled with oil the

252
00:08:21,250 --> 00:08:23,207
state already owned.

253
00:08:23,367 --> 00:08:25,006
To fill a robot fund,

254
00:08:25,006 --> 00:08:27,143
a government would first have to own

255
00:08:27,143 --> 00:08:28,545
part of the robots,

256
00:08:28,545 --> 00:08:29,857
or tax them.

257
00:08:30,057 --> 00:08:31,600
And when money gets tight,

258
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:33,614
the size of the check becomes a

259
00:08:33,614 --> 00:08:35,332
political fight.

260
00:08:35,583 --> 00:08:38,433
Some people go one step further.

261
00:08:38,483 --> 00:08:41,170
Just give everyone a basic income,

262
00:08:41,170 --> 00:08:43,003
no strings attached.

263
00:08:43,133 --> 00:08:44,401
So Rosa asks,

264
00:08:44,401 --> 00:08:46,813
has anyone actually tried?

265
00:08:46,893 --> 00:08:47,863
Yes.

266
00:08:47,863 --> 00:08:51,323
And the results surprised both sides.

267
00:08:51,583 --> 00:08:52,499
In Stockton,

268
00:08:52,499 --> 00:08:53,649
California,

269
00:08:53,649 --> 00:08:55,527
a pilot sent a small group of

270
00:08:55,527 --> 00:08:58,039
residents $500 a month,

271
00:08:58,039 --> 00:08:59,813
no strings attached.

272
00:08:59,913 --> 00:09:02,313
Plenty of critics expected people to stop

273
00:09:02,313 --> 00:09:03,313
working.

274
00:09:03,353 --> 00:09:04,140
Instead,

275
00:09:04,140 --> 00:09:05,469
after a year,

276
00:09:05,469 --> 00:09:07,639
more of them had full-time jobs than

277
00:09:07,639 --> 00:09:11,222
in a comparison group that got nothing.

278
00:09:11,483 --> 00:09:13,033
Then came a much bigger,

279
00:09:13,033 --> 00:09:15,257
carefully controlled study,

280
00:09:15,257 --> 00:09:17,054
run by a research group called

281
00:09:17,054 --> 00:09:18,473
OpenResearch.

282
00:09:18,473 --> 00:09:20,758
It sent low-income people a thousand

283
00:09:20,758 --> 00:09:21,878
dollars a month,

284
00:09:21,878 --> 00:09:22,541
for years,

285
00:09:22,541 --> 00:09:24,826
and compared them with people who got

286
00:09:24,826 --> 00:09:26,340
almost nothing.

287
00:09:26,583 --> 00:09:27,208
This time,

288
00:09:27,208 --> 00:09:29,126
people worked a little less,

289
00:09:29,126 --> 00:09:31,282
on average about an hour or two

290
00:09:31,282 --> 00:09:32,313
a week.

291
00:09:32,493 --> 00:09:33,825
They spent more on everyday needs,

292
00:09:33,825 --> 00:09:35,113
and they had more choices.

293
00:09:35,113 --> 00:09:37,123
But the money didn't lead to better

294
00:09:37,123 --> 00:09:37,547
jobs,

295
00:09:37,547 --> 00:09:39,763
and the happiness boost faded.

296
00:09:39,763 --> 00:09:40,194
So,

297
00:09:40,194 --> 00:09:41,079
a cushion,

298
00:09:41,079 --> 00:09:42,360
not a cure.

299
00:09:42,617 --> 00:09:43,907
The good reason?

300
00:09:43,907 --> 00:09:45,961
A floor that nobody falls through,

301
00:09:45,961 --> 00:09:48,127
even if work disappears.

302
00:09:48,127 --> 00:09:49,117
The catch?

303
00:09:49,117 --> 00:09:51,600
Paying everyone is enormously expensive,

304
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:53,140
and that money has to come from

305
00:09:53,140 --> 00:09:54,317
somewhere.

306
00:09:54,367 --> 00:09:56,421
Which brings Rosa right back to her

307
00:09:56,421 --> 00:09:57,792
first question.

308
00:10:02,250 --> 00:10:04,664
Let me draw the whole puzzle on

309
00:10:04,664 --> 00:10:05,790
one page.

310
00:10:05,790 --> 00:10:06,772
On the left,

311
00:10:06,772 --> 00:10:08,510
the things we'll still need.

312
00:10:08,510 --> 00:10:09,125
Schools,

313
00:10:09,125 --> 00:10:09,741
roads,

314
00:10:09,741 --> 00:10:11,960
checks for retirees.

315
00:10:12,010 --> 00:10:13,031
On the right,

316
00:10:13,031 --> 00:10:15,388
the places where AI's wealth will pile

317
00:10:15,388 --> 00:10:16,110
up.

318
00:10:16,110 --> 00:10:16,729
Profits,

319
00:10:16,729 --> 00:10:17,347
shopping,

320
00:10:17,347 --> 00:10:19,420
and the robots themselves.

321
00:10:19,420 --> 00:10:22,131
Every proposal is just a different pipe

322
00:10:22,131 --> 00:10:24,190
from the right side to the left.

323
00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:26,237
The real question isn't whether to build

324
00:10:26,237 --> 00:10:27,120
a pipe.

325
00:10:27,120 --> 00:10:28,124
It's which one,

326
00:10:28,124 --> 00:10:30,525
and who's standing under it.

327
00:10:30,783 --> 00:10:31,290
So,

328
00:10:31,290 --> 00:10:33,399
if AI replaces workers,

329
00:10:33,399 --> 00:10:35,283
who pays the taxes?

330
00:10:35,283 --> 00:10:36,617
The honest answer is,

331
00:10:36,617 --> 00:10:38,683
nobody has decided yet.

332
00:10:38,683 --> 00:10:39,318
Today,

333
00:10:39,318 --> 00:10:40,983
it's mostly workers.

334
00:10:40,983 --> 00:10:41,860
If work shrinks,

335
00:10:41,860 --> 00:10:43,187
the bill has to move,

336
00:10:43,187 --> 00:10:44,064
to profits,

337
00:10:44,064 --> 00:10:44,942
to shopping,

338
00:10:44,942 --> 00:10:46,943
to the owners of the robots,

339
00:10:46,943 --> 00:10:48,893
or to some mix of them.

340
00:10:49,033 --> 00:10:51,174
Every option has a good reason and

341
00:10:51,174 --> 00:10:52,163
a catch.

342
00:10:52,183 --> 00:10:53,262
And in the end,

343
00:10:53,262 --> 00:10:55,683
the choice isn't really about robots.

344
00:10:55,683 --> 00:10:58,466
It's about which people carry the bill.

345
00:10:58,717 --> 00:11:00,877
And there's a clock ticking.

346
00:11:00,907 --> 00:11:03,824
Social Security's reserve is projected to

347
00:11:03,824 --> 00:11:06,135
run dry in 2034,

348
00:11:06,135 --> 00:11:08,154
long before robots could take most of

349
00:11:08,154 --> 00:11:08,805
the jobs,

350
00:11:08,805 --> 00:11:10,527
if they ever do.

351
00:11:10,857 --> 00:11:13,343
So this debate may well arrive before

352
00:11:13,343 --> 00:11:14,938
the robots do.

353
00:11:15,183 --> 00:11:16,720
Back in Pinewick,

354
00:11:16,720 --> 00:11:19,113
Rosa calls a town meeting.

355
00:11:19,113 --> 00:11:20,152
She brings Stub,

356
00:11:20,152 --> 00:11:22,083
and she brings Rivet.

357
00:11:22,083 --> 00:11:23,556
Here's what we'll still need,

358
00:11:23,556 --> 00:11:24,443
she says.

359
00:11:24,443 --> 00:11:26,603
And here's who could pay for it.

360
00:11:26,773 --> 00:11:28,205
Let's decide together,

361
00:11:28,205 --> 00:11:30,673
while the paychecks are still flowing.

362
00:11:30,823 --> 00:11:32,953
Rivet raises a hand.

363
00:11:32,993 --> 00:11:34,503
Do I get a vote?

364
00:11:34,563 --> 00:11:35,197
Not yet,

365
00:11:35,197 --> 00:11:36,333
says Rosa.

366
00:11:36,333 --> 00:11:38,364
But you might get a bill.

367
00:11:38,617 --> 00:11:39,286
Next time,

368
00:11:39,286 --> 00:11:42,283
we follow the money behind another hidden

369
00:11:42,283 --> 00:11:44,727
link of the world economy.

370
00:11:44,877 --> 00:11:47,404
This is The Hidden Economics.
